WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.850
James Cullen Bressack: I also find it interesting
as well on the flip side because my auto-dm

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.060
goes out [Automated Direct Message) and says
“Hey, check out my movies on Amazon.”

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.060
or whatever.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.120
And I’ll get responses from all these people
around the world saying “Hey, I love your

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.120
movie.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.090
I saw this one or that.”

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.670
And everyone is always responding saying ‘I
love them’ or ‘I’ve seen them.’

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:26.850
And my response is either A) A distributor
owes me a lot of money that I don’t know

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:41.820
about or B) (which I think is the more likely
one) is that things are probably getting downloaded

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.680
on the Internet a lot.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:53.410
A perfect instance of piracy is a movie I
did…I won’t say which one but the first

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.800
month that it was out, it only shipped like
5,000 units.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:04.490
And then I looked at one of those piracy websites
and it had something like 150,000 downloads.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.530
And I was like “Wow!

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:12.240
If I only had like a dollar for every single
time that this happened, I’d be”…but

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.430
at least they’re watching them right?

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.790
Film Courage: I’m not advocating piracy
but do you think it can sometimes help someone’s

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.190
career or a project in some way?

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.190
James Cullen Bressack: I think sometimes.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:24.470
I mean if you look at that movie INK, if you
saw that?

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.240
They actually released it for free online.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.360
They encouraged the piracy of it.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.900
So I think yes and no.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.760
Personally I’m against piracy because I
think it’s stealing.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.040
Legitimately stealing from the filmmaker.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:43.790
And I know it’s kind of weird to say but
it’s like messed up to pirate stuff.

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:47.790
But if you’re going to pirate stuff, at
least don’t pirate the indie film.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:55.240
Like the really low budget indie stuff, the
difference between one extra DVD and one extra

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:01.840
download is somebody rent that month, possibly.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:07.850
So it’s like I feel for people because I
have a lot of friends that are at this and

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:12.640
haven’t really been doing well because of
piracy.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.660
So I want people to understand that it really
does hurt the filmmaker.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.260
It really does.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:24.300
Film Courage: How do you think a lot of people
in your generation and slightly older feel

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.290
about this because they have gotten so much
for free on the Internet [to now put parameters

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:28.290
in place for force payment]?

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.290
How do they view it?

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.780
James Cullen Bressack: I mean the weird thing
about it is I will be talking to friends from

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:39.840
high school or childhood, elementary school
or whatever, people I’ve known for years

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.840
and years.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.310
And I’ll be like “Hey, you should check
out my new movie.”

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.340
And they’ll be like “Oh, yeah.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:45.340
Sure.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.340
I’ll just download it.”

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.459
or they’ll be like “Oh yeah, I’ll watch
it on this website or whatever.”

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:53.090
And I’ll be like “You’re literally just
telling me you’re going to steal from me.”

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.250
Are you really just going to be like “Oh
yeah, I’m going to take $20.00 out of your

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.250
wallet.”

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:58.980
No!

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:05.090
But for some reason it’s so okay amongst
that…they actually think that it’s become

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:09.630
so part of the culture so that it’s fine
when it’s really not.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:16.650
So if there is a way to monetize that, such
as if we release movies for free online with

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:22.760
ads on them or something, that is at least
better than having them stolen.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:28.000
You look at those websites that have those
movies hosted for free and all this traffic

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.660
is driven to the website, they’re making
money on those sites because they are selling

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.459
ad space on those sites.

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:37.430
People are making a lot of money off of our
hard work.

